[data-question-highlight-id="question-marker-R16ajmlfiv5tpfiv5uadb"]::highlight(question-marker-R16ajmlfiv5tpfiv5uadb-green) { background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 65%, #fff); color: #172033; }
[data-question-highlight-id="question-marker-R16ajmlfiv5tpfiv5uadb"]::highlight(question-marker-R16ajmlfiv5tpfiv5uadb-pink) { background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 35%, #fff); color: #172033; }Why D is correct. Effective human handoff requires a structured summary that front-loads exactly what the human needs: the problem, confirmed identifiers, what was tried, and a recommendation. That is what removes the re-reading burden and shortens handle time. Dumping the full transcript (A) is the current failure mode — it forces the human to reconstruct the case themselves. Extended thinking (B) improves the model's own reasoning but produces no artifact for the human. Escalating earlier (C) changes when tickets arrive, not the missing information problem, and would likely lower first-contact resolution.

